"""Compare coverage runs, and measure the coverage of a patch.

generate-coverage-report answers "what is covered". This answers "is the code I just wrote
tested" -- which is the question review actually asks, and the one llvm-cov cannot answer
at all because it has nothing to compare against.

Two modes, and the first one is the one to reach for.

PATCH COVERAGE needs one trace and one diff, so it needs only the test run you were going
to do anyway:

    Tools/Scripts/generate-coverage-report --coverage-dir=/tmp/cov --output-dir=/tmp/after
    Tools/Scripts/compare-coverage-reports --current=/tmp/after/coverage.lcov.gz \\
        --git-diff=main --fail-under-patch=70

It reports the coverage of the lines the diff added or modified, and names the added lines
nothing executed. Line-number drift cannot affect it: the diff and the trace describe the
same text, so there is no second set of line numbers to shift against.

DELTA COVERAGE needs a baseline, so it needs a second full test run, and it answers a
weaker question: did the total move?

    Tools/Scripts/compare-coverage-reports --baseline=/tmp/before/coverage.lcov.gz \\
        --current=/tmp/after/coverage.lcov.gz --git-diff=main --output-dir=/tmp/delta

The two combine, and with --git-diff both are reported from one diff. But note what the
delta alone cannot see: twenty never-executed lines appended to the real Document.cpp
record -- 8,051 instrumented lines at 92.88%, an entirely untested new function -- move it
to 92.65%, so --fail-under-delta=0.5 passes. Patch coverage calls the same addition 0% of
20.

Two kinds of finding are reported by the delta, separately and never added together,
because they call for different fixes:

  * a regressed line was in both traces, executed in the baseline and not now. An
    existing test stopped reaching existing code.
  * an uncovered new line is only in the current trace and was never executed. New code
    that nothing tests.

Both are attributed by line NUMBER, because that is all lcov records. That is exact for a
file whose source did not change between the two runs, and approximate for one that did:
inserting a line shifts every line below it, and the shifted numbers then look like a mix
of regressions and new lines even though the coverage did not change. So for a file the
change touched, take the delta's line-level attribution as a pointer to where to look, and
the patch-coverage number as the one that means something. Functions are matched by
mangled name instead and do survive an edit.
"""

import logging
import optparse
import os
import sys

from webkitpy.common.system.filesystem import FileSystem
from webkitpy.common.webkit_finder import WebKitFinder
from webkitpy.coverage_delta import (absolute_paths, compare, format_summary, load_traces,
                                     write_delta_report)
from webkitpy.coverage_patch import (PatchCoverage, format_patch_summary, git_diff_added_lines,
                                     write_patch_report)
from webkitpy.coverage_provenance import read_trace_comment
from webkitpy.coverage_scope import scope_from_provenance
from webkitpy.coverage_thresholds import COVERAGE_GATE_EXIT_CODE

_log = logging.getLogger('compare-coverage-reports')

# --fail-under-delta is a float compared against a float, and a delta computed from equal
# integer ratios is not always exactly 0.0, so leave room for the last bit rather than
# failing a build over one. --fail-under-patch is compared the same way.
_TOLERANCE = 1e-9

# optparse prints this after the option list. It repeats the docstring's caveat because
# nobody reads the docstring, and a reviewer who trusts a per-line delta number for a file
# whose source moved will chase a regression that never happened.
_EPILOG = """
PATCH COVERAGE, with --current and --git-diff and no baseline, is the useful half and the
cheap one. It intersects the diff's added and modified lines with the trace's per-line
records and reports how many of them a test executed, plus the line numbers of the ones
none did. Added lines with no coverage record -- comments, blank lines, braces,
declarations -- are excluded from the denominator rather than counted uncovered, since no
test could execute them. Untracked files are included, and every line of one is an added
line. Nothing here depends on matching two sets of line numbers, so a line shift cannot
distort it. It does assume the trace was produced from the source the diff describes: an
lcov trace records no revision, so one from an older tree reads the right line numbers of
the wrong text.

DELTA COVERAGE, with --baseline as well, counts two findings separately and never adds
them together: a REGRESSED line was in both traces, executed in the baseline and not now,
so an existing test stopped reaching existing code; an UNCOVERED NEW line is only in the
current trace and was never executed, so it is new code that nothing tests. They call for
different fixes.

Both of those are attributed by line NUMBER, because that is all lcov records. That is
exact for a file whose source did not change between the two runs and only approximate for
one that did, since inserting a line shifts every line below it and the shifted numbers
then look like a mixture of regressions and new lines. Prefer the patch-coverage number for
a file the change touched. Functions are matched by mangled name instead, so those do
survive an edit.

--changed-files restricts the report to a list of files and carries no line numbers, so it
is FILE-LEVEL only: it can say how well tested the files you touched are, and it cannot say
anything about the lines you added. --git-diff is line-level as well as file-level.

A SELECTIVE trace -- one from a run over part of the suite, which the trace's own provenance
records -- is treated differently by the two modes, because the mathematics differs. Adding
tests can only turn a line from uncovered to covered, so a covered line in a subset run is
exact and an uncovered line is unknown.

  * DELTA coverage is refused. A selective current against a full baseline reports every line
    the subset did not reach as a regression, a fabricated regression the size of the tests
    that did not run. Two selective traces are compared only when their recorded test-name
    digests are equal, which is the only available proof that they ran the same tests.
  * PATCH coverage is allowed, and is sound but not complete: a line it calls covered really
    was executed, so it can raise a false alarm and it cannot grant a false pass. That is the
    right direction for a gate, so --fail-under-patch works on a selective trace and says so.
"""


class _PreformattedEpilog(optparse.IndentedHelpFormatter):
    """optparse rewraps the epilog into one blob, and the paragraph breaks carry meaning."""

    def format_epilog(self, epilog):
        return epilog or ''


def changed_files_from_file(source_root, path):
    with open(path) as handle:
        return absolute_paths(handle, source_root)


def parse_args(args):
    parser = optparse.OptionParser(usage='%prog --current=FILE [--baseline=FILE] [options]',
                                   epilog=_EPILOG, formatter=_PreformattedEpilog())
    parser.add_option('--current', help='lcov trace from after the change, gzipped or not')
    parser.add_option('--baseline', help='lcov trace from before the change, gzipped or not. '
                                         'Optional: without it, --git-diff still gives patch '
                                         'coverage of the lines the change added, off the one '
                                         'trace and so off one test run')
    parser.add_option('--baseline-source-root', metavar='DIR',
                      help='The checkout the baseline trace was produced in, if it was not '
                           'this one. Its paths are rebased onto --source-root before '
                           'anything is compared. A baseline needs no binaries, so this is '
                           'what makes a kept, downloaded or bot-produced one usable')
    parser.add_option('--output-dir', help='Directory to write the report into. Nothing is '
                                           'opened in a browser; the path is printed')
    parser.add_option('--changed-files', metavar='FILE',
                      help='File listing the changed paths, one per line, absolute or relative '
                           'to the checkout. FILE-LEVEL only -- it carries no line numbers, so '
                           'it restricts which files are reported and gated but cannot measure '
                           'the lines you added. Use --git-diff for that')
    parser.add_option('--git-diff', metavar='REF',
                      help='Derive the change from git diff -U0 REF, hunks and all. This is '
                           'what makes the tool usable in review: it gives both the file list '
                           'and the added line numbers patch coverage needs. `main` means '
                           'everything since main including the working tree and untracked '
                           'files, `main...HEAD` only the commits. Combines with '
                           '--changed-files')
    parser.add_option('--source-root', help='Top of the checkout, used to canonicalize copied '
                                            'header paths in both traces and to resolve relative '
                                            'changed-file paths. Defaults to this checkout')
    parser.add_option('--fail-under-patch', type='float', metavar='PERCENT',
                      help='Exit {} if fewer than PERCENT of the added lines that have '
                           'coverage data were executed. Needs --git-diff, since only a diff '
                           'carries line numbers. A change that added no instrumented lines '
                           'at all passes'.format(COVERAGE_GATE_EXIT_CODE))
    parser.add_option('--fail-under-delta', type='float', metavar='PERCENT',
                      help='Exit {} if line coverage fell by more than PERCENT percentage '
                           'points, so this can gate CI. 0 fails on any drop at all. Needs '
                           '--baseline. The gate is on the percentage, over whichever files '
                           'the report covers; line-level regressions are reported but do not '
                           'fail it by themselves. It cannot see a wholly untested addition to '
                           'a large well-covered file, which --fail-under-patch can. '
                           'For a threshold on absolute coverage rather than on the change, see '
                           "generate-coverage-report's --fail-under-lines".format(
                               COVERAGE_GATE_EXIT_CODE))
    parser.add_option('--max-files', type='int', default=25, metavar='N',
                      help='Files to list in the text summary. The HTML report always lists all '
                           'of them (default: %default)')
    parser.add_option('-v', '--verbose', action='store_true', default=False, help='Verbose logging')

    options, remaining = parser.parse_args(args)
    if remaining:
        parser.error('Unexpected arguments: {}'.format(' '.join(remaining)))
    if not options.current:
        parser.error('--current is required')
    for name in ('current', 'baseline', 'changed_files'):
        value = getattr(options, name)
        if value and not os.path.isfile(value):
            parser.error('--{} {} does not exist'.format(name.replace('_', '-'), value))
    if not options.baseline and not (options.git_diff or options.changed_files):
        # Without a baseline there is no comparison, so the change has to be named some other
        # way or there is nothing here that generate-coverage-report does not already do.
        parser.error('Without --baseline there is nothing to compare against, so name the '
                     'change: --git-diff=REF for patch coverage of the lines it added, or '
                     '--changed-files=FILE for a file-level view')
    if options.baseline_source_root and not options.baseline:
        parser.error('--baseline-source-root only makes sense with --baseline')
    if options.fail_under_delta is not None:
        if not options.baseline:
            # It used to warn and exit 0 in every case it could not evaluate itself. Refusing
            # up front is the same fix applied one step earlier.
            parser.error('--fail-under-delta compares against a baseline, so it needs '
                         '--baseline. For a gate that needs only this run, use '
                         '--fail-under-patch')
        if options.fail_under_delta < 0:
            parser.error('--fail-under-delta is a size, so it cannot be negative')
    if options.fail_under_patch is not None:
        if not options.git_diff:
            parser.error('--fail-under-patch measures the lines the change added, so it needs '
                         '--git-diff. --changed-files carries no line numbers')
        if not 0 <= options.fail_under_patch <= 100:
            parser.error('--fail-under-patch is a percentage, so it has to be between 0 and 100')
    return options


def trace_scope(path):
    """The CoverageScope a trace records, from the provenance comment in its own head.

    A trace with no comment -- one from before provenance existed, or one produced by another
    tool -- reads as full-suite, which is what such a trace was in practice: there was no way to
    produce a selective one that said so.
    """
    return scope_from_provenance(read_trace_comment(path))


def report_delta(options, source_root, baseline_files, current_files, changed_files):
    """Print and write the delta report. 1 if it could not be made, 2 if its gate fired."""
    delta = compare(baseline_files, current_files, changed_files)
    if delta.ignored_path_count:
        _log.info('Ignored %d changed path(s) that are not C, C++ or Objective-C sources',
                  delta.ignored_path_count)
    if changed_files and not delta.scope_paths:
        # Distinguishing these two matters: the first is a normal outcome for a change that
        # only touched tests, and the second usually means --source-root is wrong.
        if delta.ignored_path_count == len(changed_files):
            _log.error('None of the changed paths are C, C++ or Objective-C sources, so none '
                       'of them can have coverage data.')
        else:
            _log.error('None of the changed source files appear in either trace, so there is '
                       'nothing to compare. Was the build instrumented, and does --source-root '
                       'match the paths in the traces?')
        return 1

    summary = format_summary(delta, source_root=source_root, max_files=options.max_files)
    print(summary, end='')

    if options.output_dir:
        page = write_delta_report(delta, options.output_dir, source_root=source_root)
        # Deliberately not opened: a tool that launches a browser is unusable from a build
        # bot and was an explicit review objection on https://webkit.org/b/206389.
        with open(os.path.join(options.output_dir, 'summary.txt'), 'w') as handle:
            handle.write(summary)
        _log.info('Wrote %s', page)

    if options.fail_under_delta is None:
        return 0

    # The gate follows the report's scope, so `--changed-files X --fail-under-delta 0` reads
    # as "the files I touched must not lose coverage". Note that scope includes the
    # denominator: a file that grows by untested lines drops its own percentage without any
    # line regressing, and with an allowance of 0 that fails too, which is the point.
    scope = ('the {} changed file(s) with coverage data'.format(delta.scope.file_count)
             if delta.focused else 'the whole project')
    change = delta.scope.percent_delta()
    if change is None:
        # It used to warn here and return 0, which made it a gate-shaped object rather than a
        # gate: with a baseline rooted in another checkout, every file reads as new, the
        # baseline contributes nothing, and this is the branch that was taken -- so the tool
        # said "cannot be applied" once, at WARNING, and exited 0. A gate that cannot evaluate
        # itself has to fail, the same rule coverage_thresholds applies to a metric with
        # nothing to measure.
        _log.error('There is no baseline line coverage percentage for %s, so '
                   '--fail-under-delta cannot be evaluated and is failing rather than '
                   'silently passing. Do the two traces describe the same files?', scope)
        return COVERAGE_GATE_EXIT_CODE
    if change < -options.fail_under_delta - _TOLERANCE:
        _log.error('Line coverage over %s fell by %.2fpp, more than the %.2fpp allowed by '
                   '--fail-under-delta.', scope, -change, options.fail_under_delta)
        return COVERAGE_GATE_EXIT_CODE
    _log.info('Line coverage over %s changed by %+.2fpp, within the %.2fpp allowed by '
              '--fail-under-delta.', scope, change, options.fail_under_delta)
    return 0


def report_patch(options, source_root, added_lines, current_files, line_numbers=True,
                 scope=None):
    """Print and write the patch report. 1 if it could not be made, 2 if its gate fired."""
    patch = PatchCoverage(added_lines, current_files, line_numbers=line_numbers)
    if patch.ignored_path_count:
        _log.info('Ignored %d changed path(s) that are not C, C++ or Objective-C sources',
                  patch.ignored_path_count)
    if not patch.files:
        _log.error('None of the changed source files have coverage data in %s, so there is '
                   'nothing to measure. Was the build instrumented, does --source-root match '
                   'the paths in the trace, and did the run exercise these binaries?',
                   options.current)
        return 1

    summary = format_patch_summary(patch, source_root=source_root, max_files=options.max_files)
    if scope is not None and scope.is_selective:
        # Above the number, not below it: patch coverage under selection is a lower bound, so an
        # uncovered added line here may be a line no test that ran reached rather than a line no
        # test covers. The uncovered line list is still exactly the thing to act on -- it is a
        # superset of the truly untested lines and never a subset.
        summary = '\n'.join(scope.banner_lines() + ['', summary])
    print(summary, end='')
    if options.output_dir:
        name = 'patch-summary.txt' if line_numbers else 'changed-files-summary.txt'
        path = os.path.join(options.output_dir, name)
        with open(path, 'w') as handle:
            handle.write(summary)
        _log.info('Wrote %s', path)
        # A coverage report already in the output directory is what the line numbers link into,
        # so its presence is the whole test: with it, an uncovered line number is a link to that
        # line of the annotated source, and without it there is nothing to link to and the
        # numbers stay text. webkit-coverage points --output-dir at the report directory, which
        # is the case that matters.
        report_root = '' if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(options.output_dir, 'index.html')) else None
        page = write_patch_report(patch, options.output_dir, source_root=source_root,
                                  report_root=report_root, max_files=options.max_files,
                                  scope=scope)
        _log.info('Wrote %s%s', page,
                  '' if report_root is not None else
                  ' (uncovered line numbers are not links: no coverage report beside it to '
                  'link into)')

    if options.fail_under_patch is None:
        return 0
    if not patch.instrumented_line_count:
        # Not a gate that could not be evaluated: the change genuinely added no line any test
        # could execute -- comments, declarations, a header rename -- and there is nothing for
        # a test to reach. Failing that would make the flag unusable on a real branch.
        _log.info('The change added no lines with coverage data, so there is nothing for '
                  '--fail-under-patch to require.')
        return 0
    percent = patch.percent()
    if percent < options.fail_under_patch - _TOLERANCE:
        _log.error('Patch coverage is %.2f%% (%d of %d added lines with coverage data), below '
                   'the %.2f%% required by --fail-under-patch.', percent,
                   patch.covered_line_count, patch.instrumented_line_count,
                   options.fail_under_patch)
        if scope is not None and scope.is_selective:
            _log.error('This is a selective run, so that is a lower bound: the gate is sound '
                       'but not complete here, and this may be a false alarm. Every line it '
                       'names as covered really was executed.')
        return COVERAGE_GATE_EXIT_CODE
    _log.info('Patch coverage is %.2f%% (%d of %d added lines with coverage data), at or above '
              'the %.2f%% required by --fail-under-patch.', percent, patch.covered_line_count,
              patch.instrumented_line_count, options.fail_under_patch)
    if scope is not None and scope.is_selective:
        # Worth saying on a pass as well as on a failure: this direction is the sound one, and a
        # reader who does not know that will discount the pass along with the number.
        _log.info('This is a selective run, so that is a lower bound -- which means the pass is '
                  'not a false one: adding the tests that did not run can only raise it.')
    return 0


def main(args):
    options = parse_args(args)
    logging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG if options.verbose else logging.INFO,
                        format='%(levelname)s: %(message)s')

    source_root = options.source_root or WebKitFinder(FileSystem()).webkit_base()

    added_lines = None
    changed_files = None
    if options.changed_files or options.git_diff:
        changed_files = set()
        try:
            if options.changed_files:
                changed_files |= changed_files_from_file(source_root, options.changed_files)
            if options.git_diff:
                # One git invocation for both the file list and the line numbers, so the two
                # cannot disagree about what the change is. This is also where untracked files
                # enter: git diff --name-only never mentioned them, so a brand-new file used
                # to be missing from the scope entirely.
                added_lines = git_diff_added_lines(source_root, options.git_diff)
                changed_files |= set(added_lines)
        except (EnvironmentError, RuntimeError) as error:
            # A mistyped ref is the most likely way to get here, and a traceback would say
            # much less about it than the message git already produced.
            _log.error('%s', error)
            return 1
        if not changed_files:
            _log.error('No changed files to compare.')
            return 1
        _log.info('Restricting the comparison to %d changed path(s)', len(changed_files))

    # Read before the traces are parsed, which for a full-suite pair is 5.4 s and 0.98 GB each:
    # a comparison this refuses should not first spend ten seconds loading what it will not use.
    current_scope = trace_scope(options.current)
    if current_scope.is_selective:
        for line in current_scope.banner_lines():
            _log.info('%s', line)
    if options.baseline:
        baseline_scope = trace_scope(options.baseline)
        refusal = current_scope.comparison_refusal(baseline_scope)
        if refusal:
            _log.error('Refusing to compute a delta: %s', refusal)
            _log.error('Patch coverage needs no baseline and is sound under selection, so use '
                       '--current with --git-diff and no --baseline instead.')
            return 1

    try:
        baseline_files, current_files = load_traces(
            options.current, options.baseline, source_root=source_root,
            baseline_source_root=options.baseline_source_root)
    except RuntimeError as error:
        _log.error('%s', error)
        return 1

    if options.output_dir:
        os.makedirs(options.output_dir, exist_ok=True)

    status = 0
    if baseline_files is not None:
        code = report_delta(options, source_root, baseline_files, current_files, changed_files)
        if code == 1:
            return 1
        status = max(status, code)
    if added_lines is not None:
        code = report_patch(options, source_root, added_lines, current_files,
                            scope=current_scope)
        if code == 1:
            return 1
        status = max(status, code)
    elif baseline_files is None:
        # --current and --changed-files with no diff and no baseline. A list of paths carries
        # no line numbers, so the only thing left to report is the file-level view, which is
        # what --baseline=X --current=X used to be a way of asking for.
        code = report_patch(options, source_root, {path: [] for path in changed_files},
                            current_files, line_numbers=False, scope=current_scope)
        if code == 1:
            return 1
        status = max(status, code)
    return status


if __name__ == '__main__':
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
